    {
      "object_id": "mathia_interpretation_043cce6fb05d19b0cef6561d9d5d5bb2abdb9f5750486bf71a2139083d984394",
      "object_role": "interpretation",
      "proposed_content": "Hasse evaluates a proof not only by whether it reaches the elliptic result but by the kind of theory it organizes: he treats arithmetic of one-variable function fields over finite constants as the adequate pRH framework and prefers the second proof's purely structural setup over the first proof's restrictive assumptions. Interpreted as methodology, this preference makes a representation reusable enough to suggest higher-genus routes, but the span does not show that structural form transfers the proof or contains the missing mathematics.",
