    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_475ad66bc71f2634680de50bcf342094d3576e77e62d4d3c7eb1dd93d9269c9f",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Reduction from a controlled characteristic-zero CM curve is first visible in the special Gauss-Herglotz example and becomes Hasse's general elliptic proof strategy; Deuring's theorem repairs Hasse's exceptional elliptic cases by lifting the curve together with the relevant endomorphism, whereas higher-dimensional pair lifting needs ordinarity-type hypotheses and general CM lifting survives only after passage to a suitable isogenous representative.",
